<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jataka tales</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jataka tales are a large body of stories about the previous births of the Buddha, in which he appears as a human or an animal practising the virtues that lead to enlightenment. The Pali collection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> canon contains 547 tales, many set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> under kings named Brahmadatta. Carved on stupas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a> and Sanchi and retold across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, they are also a source on ancient Indian society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jataka-tales.jpg" alt="Jataka tales" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southern_India,_Andhra_Pradesh,_possibly_Amaravati,_Satavahana_period_-_Buddha%27s_Previous_Life_as_a_Woodpecker-_Javasakunda_Jataka_-_1970.350_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
